116: | Operation | Required result rule | Optional result rule |
117: | --- | --- | --- |
118: | Ordinary conditional | Every reachable arm produces a required value. | At least one reachable arm can be null, and the destination permits null. |
119: | Nil-merge | The guarded target is already required, or the fallback supplies a required value when the target is null. | Both target and fallback can be null, and the destination permits null. |
120: | Parameter with native default | The selected body type and operation establish a required value. | The selected body type remains nullable; native placement supplies no additional proof. |
121: | Entry-normalized parameter | The normalization's possible results are all required. | A permitted normalization result remains nullable. |
122: 
123: Present alternatives use the common destination container representation.
124: A null literal represents an absent value. Empty literals
125: receive their element type and constructor from the result context. Do not
126: accept incompatible alternatives by copying one arm's storage field.
127: 
128: For nil-merge recognition, the arm returning the guarded target must be the
129: non-null outcome of the comparison. Account for both `==` and `!=`, either
130: comparison operand order, and either branch orientation. Reuse the existing
131: structural decision across its consumers, with one owner for recognition.
132: Its stability precondition must justify any eliminated repeated source read.
133: Otherwise preserve the general conditional form.
134: 
135: An optional nil-merge target can remain optional during preparation even when
136: the final result is required: the fallback may supply the required result.
137: Do not force that target through a required-destination conversion before the
138: merge has established presence. A prior valid guard can instead make the
139: prepared target required, in which case the fallback is unreachable. Source
140: `Null<T>` spelling alone decides neither case.
